; Ghost Run: a time trial on stunt tracks high above the fields. Every run races against your best one, which
; drives next to you as a see-through ghost.
;
; Every picture and sound in this game is made by the code itself, so there are no files to ship with it.
;
; Controls:
;   Up or W          accelerate
;   Down or S        brake, and reverse once stopped
;   Left/Right, A/D  steer, in the air too
;   Space            handbrake: the back of the car slides out, for drifting round tight corners
;   Backspace        back to the last checkpoint (the clock keeps running)
;   R                start the run again
;   Escape or P      the pause menu
;   Menus            Up/Down or the mouse choose, Enter/Space or a click select, Left/Right change an option
;
; How it works:
; - A track is written as a short line of pieces, such as "S2 L2 J3": two straights, a left turn of 90 degrees and a
;   jump. The pieces move a "turtle" forward, and every 2 units it leaves a slice: a point with the directions
;   forward, right and up. The road, its barriers and the gates are built from these slices.
; - The road and barriers become one static triangle mesh in blitzphysics. The car is not a physics body at all: four
;   rays (PhysicsRayCast) find the road under the wheels and work as springs, and a sphere (PhysicsSphereCast) moves
;   the car and slides it along whatever it would hit. That is all an arcade car needs, and it never tips over.
; - The ghost is a recording: where the car was and how it was turned, 60 times a second. The best run of each track
;   is saved next to the program, with the times it passed every checkpoint, so the game can show how far ahead or
;   behind you are.
;
; The options and best times are saved in ghost-run.cfg, the ghosts in ghost-run-1.ghost and so on.

